00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.920
And now, on with the show!

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.060
Finding Dory comes up short in at least four
key areas where FINDING NEMO excels.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:17.030
For the sake of clarity, let’s categorize
these problems as: 1) Clouded emotional investment,

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:23.970
2) shaky narrative structure, 3) passive heroism,
4) purposeless entourage.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:28.470
To be fair, these issues are almost impossible
to separate from one another, so there will

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.370
inevitably be some overlap.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:31.620
But here we go, anyway...

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.830
First: the question of emotional investment.

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:38.760
A key objective of any screenplay is to get
the audience to care deeply about the protagonist

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.880
a goal that’s often achieved by first getting
us to care about whatever our protagonist

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.140
most values and/or needs.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:49.080
Maybe the protagonist is focused on scoring
an internship...or going the distance in

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.740
the boxing ring...or on breaking free of a
suffocating life.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:57.240
Whatever the case, in most strong screenplays,
that desire is established early.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.320
It’s singular.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:02:59.450
It’s powerful.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.349
And it generates our empathy.

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:05.900
In FINDING NEMO and FINDING DORY, the protagonists’
goals are blatantly similar.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:10.620
In the first film, Marlin searches for his
kidnapped son; in the second, Dory searches

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.090
for the parents she hazily remembers.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:16.440
Although there’s nothing wrong with leaning
on the trope of the missing family member--

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.970
I mean, it works for Liam Neeson.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:25.310
Finding Nemo and Finding Dory unfortunately
have different attitudes towards the magnitude

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:26.310
of loss.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:31.100
That’s because only one of these films--
offers us a baseline before it rips a loved one away.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:36.000
And it’s that baseline that creates emotional
connection between us, the protagonist, and

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.500
the protagonist’s need.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.260
In other words, even though the bulk of Finding
Nemo keeps Marlin and Nemo apart, it first

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.739
offers a sense of what these family members
mean to one another.

00:03:45.739 --> 00:03:50.080
As early as the first minute of Finding Nemo,
we’re told what Marlin values more than

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.950
anything else in the world: "These are our
kids we're talking about!

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.280
They deserve the best!"

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:59.010
And Marlin proves that value system through
his actions: In the film’s baseline moments,

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:04.700
we see Marlin pledge to protect his son, then tease his
son, then prepare his son for school, then bring

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:06.920
his son to school, and then lecture his son.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.380
"You're about to swim into open water!"

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:08.900
"No!"

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.960
We have a sense of history.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:16.540
So when Nemo gets kidnapped 15 minutes into
the film, we, like Marlin, have loved, and

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:17.630
we have lost.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:19.080
We are invested.

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:24.900
Finding Dory offers no such baseline, so it
never offers the gut punch delivered by its predecessor.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.240
In fact, Dory loses her parents within the
five minutes of Finding Dory.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.720
In the opening scene of the film, Dory, even
to her own parents,

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:36.300
is positioned as something of a liability. A cute little goof.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.780
"Okay sweetheart, what about the rhyme we learned?

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:41.360
We see the undertow and we say..."

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:42.360
"Let's go."

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:44.180
"No, no."

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:48.120
Shortly thereafter, like bubbles in a bathtub,
her parents vanish.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.740
How much can we really be expected to pour
our emotions into losing characters we never knew?

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:53.920
"Well where are your parents?"

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:57.140
"Ummm... I can't remember."

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:02.139
If Finding Dory can’t get much mileage out
of the parent-child bond that energized Finding

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:05.440
Nemo, then what is the central relationship
here?

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:07.340
Where do we invest our empathy?

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.759
You might guess that we feed into the bond between
Marlin and Dory.

00:05:10.759 --> 00:05:13.060
After all, they did spend a whole movie together.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:15.240
They’re probably totally buds now, right?

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.560
"You know what you can do, Dory?

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.140
You can go wait over there.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.060
Go wait over there and forget! It's what you do best."

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.360
Not really.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.759
Seems the arc of personal growth that Marlin
and Dory experienced in Finding Nemo has reverted

00:05:28.759 --> 00:05:33.039
here, with Dory now being viewed as a nuisance
by pretty much everyone.

00:05:33.039 --> 00:05:37.719
Sadly, it isn’t until Dory is separated
from the relationships she formed in the first

00:05:37.719 --> 00:05:42.360
film that we’re introduced to what is supposed
to be her source of support in this movie:

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:49.490
the, uh, bond-- between Dory and Hank, a curmudgeonly
octopus who wants only to exploit Dory for

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:51.540
the transport tag attached to her fin.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:56.340
"No, no, no. If I just take your tag, I can take your place on the transport truck."

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:58.540
And, yeah, I mean that’s pretty much it.

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:00.289
That’s the nature of their friendship.

00:06:00.289 --> 00:06:01.570
Heartwarming, right?

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:06.919
The emotionally deficient relationship between
Hank and Dory is almost entirely transactional

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:10.820
and deceitful, which doubles down on the film’s
empathy gap.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:15.920
But later, FINDING DORY hastily tries to switch
gears, and that brings us to our second point...

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:17.720
2) Shaky Narrative Structure.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:22.399
Take a look at most memorable films, and you’ll
find the protagonist making a key decision

00:06:22.399 --> 00:06:24.050
right at the center of the story.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:29.060
It’s a moment that reframes the protagonist’s
objectives and tests his or her resolve.

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:31.860
Some refer to this as the film’s “midpoint shift.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:36.719
It’s no coincidence that the halfway mark
is often a major moment of self-reflection

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.080
for the protagonist; a turning point in his
or her journey...

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:41.100
"I'm with you now."

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.660
So what happens halfway through FINDING DORY?

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:47.280
Well, Hank reveals he's got a soft spot for
that loopy blue fish

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:53.740
"You'll forget me in a heartbeat, kid. Three heartbeats. I'll have a hard time forgettin' you though."

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:56.060
...but where did this sentiment come from?

00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:00.759
It’s surely not earned by any particular
actions or moments in the film, itself, because

00:07:00.759 --> 00:07:05.409
Hank and Dory are never really a team --and,
unlike Dory and Marlin, they’re never really shown

00:07:05.409 --> 00:07:06.889
enjoying their adventure.

00:07:06.889 --> 00:07:11.039
And, hey, come to think of it, Hank isn’t
even the protagonist here!

00:07:11.039 --> 00:07:15.979
In any case, since Dory and Hank have almost
no moments of genuine connection within the

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:20.300
first half of Finding Dory, Hank’s change
of heart towards Dory, midway through the

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.720
film, can’t help but feel unearned.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.160
"I don't care."

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:28.380
Contrast this with the realization Marlin
experiences in the middle of Finding Nemo.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:30.800
"And maybe he wouldn't have done it if I hadn't been so tough on him."

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:35.340
Here, Marlin begins to recognize the error
of his overprotective parenting style.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:39.780
And this realization is a direct result of
his control-freak behavior nearly getting

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.040
Dory killed.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.160
Throughout Finding Nemo, this midpoint shift
and other character-revealing moments are

00:07:45.169 --> 00:07:47.819
directly motivated by cause and effect.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:52.100
Nemo is motivated to put his life in jeopardy
because he learns his dad is heroic.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:55.060
"Your dad's been fighting the entire ocean looking for you!"

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:56.040
"My father?"

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:59.960
Marlin is motivated to team with Dory because
he realizes Dory can read.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:01.580
"Wait a minute. You can read?"

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:03.500
"I can read? That's right! I can read!"

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:08.620
Later, Marlin is motivated to separate himself
from Dory because he thinks his son is dead.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:13.520
In Finding Dory, however, key moments in the
story, like Hank’s change of heart towards

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:17.060
Dory, are largely motivated by convenience
and necessity.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:22.720
They are simply what need to happen to move
characters from point A to point B. Later,

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:24.240
Dory refers to Hank as:

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:30.980
Even though evidence of any such sweetness
is scarce at best.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:36.500
But, hey, in this movie, being an ass is pretty
easily forgiven, as long as you pay lip service

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:37.920
to some sort of growth.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:40.300
Deep into Finding Dory, Marlin tells Dory.

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:45.880
"Ever since I met you, you've shown
me how to do stuff I've never dreamed of doing.

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:47.080
Crazy things."

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:52.000
But even this sentiment, too, seems unearned,
because it leverages moments from the first

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:56.450
film -- in which Dory and Marlin adventured
and grew closer together, rather than from

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:00.600
this sequel, in which they are either separated
from each other or annoyed.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:05.060
Nevertheless, this moment is meant to play
as a moment of self-awareness and maturity

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:06.130
from Marlin.

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:11.360
Which raises the question: what is Dory’s
key moment of self-awareness in Finding Dory?

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:16.600
"You remembered in your own amazing Dory way."

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:19.760
Wow. You’ve got to wait until the movie is nearly
over to find out!

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:22.460
And this illustrates Finding Dory’s third
setback.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:24.060
Passive Heroism.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:28.660
For much of the film’s running time Dory
operates as a passive protagonist.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:32.300
one who responds to events rather than create
opportunities.

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:38.140
Some of this tendency is due to her all-too-convenient
memory flashes-- which handily take the story

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.340
wherever the screenwriters need it to go.

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:43.220
These flashes, and even the occasional undertow,

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:48.480
are driven more by chance and proximity than
as a result of personal effort or growth.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:54.040
Although, sometimes it's almost as if the film is suggesting Dory's disability can be willed away.

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:55.100
"I remembered something!"

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:58.540
This perspective seems, um, fishy at best.

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:04.160
And it doesn’t leave Dory with many opportunities
to demonstrate her ingenuity and development.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:10.680
Events often happen to Dory, rather than because
of her, and that’s never a recipe for great storytelling.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:16.240
Of course, another cause of Dory’s passivity
is the nature of the film’s actual environment.

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:21.050
After the film's first twenty minutes, Dory
quite literally becomes a fish out of water,

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:25.500
a status which restricts her movements to
the whims of other characters.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:29.480
How much can we expect our protagonist to
be the master of her own fate when she’s

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:34.740
being carried around in coffee pots, baby
buggies, or a bucket that’s labeled “Destiny”?

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:40.180
By trading the thrilling blue ocean of the
first film to the claustrophobic, human-centric

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:45.240
clinical spaces of Finding Dory, Pixar ultimately
hamstrings the scope of the story it’s able

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:49.020
to tell, and the degree to which its protagonist
can help tell it.

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:54.890
Dory, then, isn’t a driver of her own story,
she’s a passenger -a passive responder to

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:59.660
triggered memories, an odd partnership, and,
very often, blind luck.

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:03.440
But at least her personal motivation remains
relatively clear.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:07.640
"Do you know anyone who lost a kid a long time ago that would be me?"

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.780
She wants to find her parents.

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:13.740
And so, if we’re willing to buy into the
premise of the film, we must simply want to

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:14.740
find them, too.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:19.320
Even though Dory’s desire to see her family
is very conveniently triggered by the school

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:26.740
of fish saying the word "undertow" it’s true that Dory’s
ultimate objective is never in doubt.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:29.880
But the same can’t always be said for Marlin
in this film.

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:36.980
And it’s even less clear for Nemo, who, at this point, seems in desperate need of Child Protective Services.

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:39.950
And this leads us to... The Purposeless Entourage

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:46.230
I’ll pause here, to ask a simple but important
question: why does Finding Dory involve all

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:47.970
three of these fish?

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:52.750
And does Nemo’s involvement actually negatively
impact our feelings about Dory and Marlin?

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:57.160
That’s right - Marlin, having just a year
earlier crossed the perilous ocean to save

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:02.290
his only surviving child, now agrees to drag
that same child with him to guide Dory all

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:06.790
the way across the ocean again simply because:
"How can you be talking about the view when

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:08.370
I remember my family?"

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:10.020
Um, okay.

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:15.240
But this springs from the studio’s need
for a sequel, not from clear character behavior.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:20.940
Ultimately, Finding Dory commits the sin of
asking Marlin and Nemo to undermine so much

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:23.050
of what made Finding Nemo powerful.

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:27.810
After all of the emotional investment we as
an audience made in Nemo’s recovery in Finding

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:33.100
Nemo, Finding Dory asks us to believe that
Marlin, responsible father extraordinaire,

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:37.900
is willing to risk his own son’s life on
the basis of Dory’s hunch about her parents

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:39.600
she hasn’t seen in years.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:45.600
And no, I’m not being hyperbolic: Nemo really,
seriously almost dies-- I mean, couldn’t

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:47.480
Marlin find a babysitter?

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:49.780
Why is Nemo part of this new adventure?

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:54.340
Why does Dory, who presumably cares about
Nemo’s safety, agree to pull him along on

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:57.560
a dangerous mission to serve her own personal
agenda?

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:02.760
The film mostly bypasses these questions,
and -- if we are to enjoy it -- so must we.

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:07.840
But we should all take a moment to at least
recognize the weirdness of ending a fish movie

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:09.750
with a car chase on the freeway.

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:11.510
And, hey, look at that!

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:13.920
Dory’s a passenger again.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:15.480
In her own movie.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:17.100
Thanks for watching guys. Peace!

